  "prompt_text": "This figure shows the finite-temperature phase diagram of a strongly interacting fermionic gas across the BCS–BEC crossover. The horizontal axis is the interaction parameter \\(1/\\gamma\\), running from the BCS side on the left to the BEC side on the right, while the vertical axis is temperature scaled by the Fermi temperature, \\(T/T_F\\).\n\nA solid blue curve marks the superfluid transition temperature \\(T_c\\). Below this line, the system is superfluid over the whole crossover, with sketches suggesting large overlapping Cooper pairs on the BCS side, tighter pairs near the middle, and compact bosonic molecules on the BEC side. Above \\(T_c\\), the normal state changes character. On the far left it is labeled as a Fermi liquid with unpaired fermions. Moving toward stronger attraction, a dashed red line \\(T^*\\) appears above \\(T_c\\), indicating pair formation at temperatures where phase coherence has not yet developed. The region between \\(T_c\\) and \\(T^*\\) is therefore a paired but non-superfluid regime. On the right side, the high-temperature normal phase is labeled a Bose liquid, consistent with preformed molecular pairs. Overall, the figure emphasizes the smooth evolution from fermionic to bosonic behavior.",
